WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.033
Diffusion models.

2
00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:08.967
The recipe behind modern image, video, and audio generation starts with a surprisingly simple task.

3
00:00:10.167 --> 00:00:13.200
A diffusion model is trained on one simple task.

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.467
Give it a slightly noised image, plus the noise level.

5
00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:19.653
Its job is to predict the noise that was added.

6
00:00:19.653 --> 00:00:20.267
That's it.

7
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:22.867
There are two processes.

8
00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:24.600
Start with a clean image.

9
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.597
The forward process adds a little scheduled noise at each step.

10
00:00:28.597 --> 00:00:30.533
It's fixed and needs no learning.

11
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:33.333
Until the image dissolves into pure static.

12
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.300
The reverse process is the learned part.

13
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.967
The denoiser removes a little predicted noise each pass, until an image crystallizes.

14
00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:43.567
That walk is generation.

15
00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:46.833
Here's the forward process as a mix.

16
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:51.714
As the noise level rises, the image signal fades and noise takes over.

17
00:00:51.714 --> 00:00:56.033
Training means learning to reverse one small step of exactly this.

18
00:00:57.233 --> 00:00:59.133
So why did diffusion beat GANs?

19
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:03.500
Its training is a stable regression loss, not an adversarial game.

20
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.633
It covers the whole data distribution, so there's no mode collapse.

21
00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:13.967
And step-by-step generation gives natural control hooks, which is where text conditioning plugs in.

22
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:16.367
Here it is in code.

23
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:21.467
With a pretrained pipeline, fifty denoising steps turn pure noise into an image.

24
00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:27.833
And the forward process by hand is just a blend between the image and noise, at five noise levels.

25
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:30.500
Here's a practical use.

26
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:37.300
Your exoplanet classifier starves on multi-planet transits, with only a few hundred real examples.

27
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.500
You train a small one-dimensional diffusion model and sample synthetic light curves.

28
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:50.800
Rare-class recall lifts from zero point six one to zero point seven four, and your eval discipline proves the data helped.

29
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:52.791
Let's recap.

30
00:01:52.791 --> 00:01:54.833
Train a model to predict noise.

31
00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:58.167
Forward noising is fixed, and the reverse is learned.

32
00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:02.000
Stable loss, full coverage, and control hooks beat GANs.

33
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.605
The same recipe drives image, video, and audio.

34
00:02:05.605 --> 00:02:07.433
Next up: Stable Diffusion.
